COPY public.case_files (id, cluster_id, summary, confidence_note, suggested_action, key_signals, caveats, raw_response, model, prompt_version, cluster_score_at_generation, detector_version, generated_at, cost_usd, input_tokens, output_tokens, duration_ms, usage_json) FROM stdin;
17673796-8166-4056-8f14-c18f30af6859	2f2df187-bb34-4681-bc3f-dc96b1975744	Four accounts made 21 purchases on a single day (28 Aug), all paying with the same card, and three of them ship to the same address. The shared card covers 12 of those transactions and the shared address 8. Amounts are ordinary retail sizes (3,328–8,608 INR, median 6,128). The timing looks like people clicking, not scripts: gaps average around 7.5 minutes and are irregular. One of the four accounts has three or fewer transactions, which can mean a throwaway account made for a signup discount, but three of the four look established. The obvious innocent read is a household or small group sharing one card and one delivery address during a sale day. Nothing here separates that from a discount-abuse ring yet.	Moderate but unresolved — the sharing is real and concentrated in a single day, yet a shared card plus shared address is exactly what a family looks like, and the human-paced timing argues against automation.	review_closer	["one card used by all 4 accounts across 12 transactions", "one address shared by 3 accounts across 8 transactions", "all 21 transactions on a single day", "one account with 3 or fewer transactions ever", "manual, irregular pacing (about 7.5 minutes between purchases)"]	["A household or shared flat using one card and one delivery address explains everything shown here.", "Check whether the accounts' names, phones, and emails look like distinct real people or like variations on one identity.", "Check whether first-order or referral discounts were applied — repeated new-customer discounts on one card would push this toward coordinated abuse.", "Check account ages and prior history: four accounts all created shortly before 28 Aug would be much harder to explain innocently.", "The evidence contains no device-sharing data and no history before 28 Aug, so this may be one day out of a longer normal pattern."]	{"caveats": ["A household or shared flat using one card and one delivery address explains everything shown here.", "Check whether the accounts' names, phones, and emails look like distinct real people or like variations on one identity.", "Check whether first-order or referral discounts were applied — repeated new-customer discounts on one card would push this toward coordinated abuse.", "Check account ages and prior history: four accounts all created shortly before 28 Aug would be much harder to explain innocently.", "The evidence contains no device-sharing data and no history before 28 Aug, so this may be one day out of a longer normal pattern."], "summary": "Four accounts made 21 purchases on a single day (28 Aug), all paying with the same card, and three of them ship to the same address. The shared card covers 12 of those transactions and the shared address 8. Amounts are ordinary retail sizes (3,328–8,608 INR, median 6,128). The timing looks like people clicking, not scripts: gaps average around 7.5 minutes and are irregular. One of the four accounts has three or fewer transactions, which can mean a throwaway account made for a signup discount, but three of the four look established. The obvious innocent read is a household or small group sharing one card and one delivery address during a sale day. Nothing here separates that from a discount-abuse ring yet.", "confidence": "Moderate but unresolved — the sharing is real and concentrated in a single day, yet a shared card plus shared address is exactly what a family looks like, and the human-paced timing argues against automation.", "key_signals": ["one card used by all 4 accounts across 12 transactions", "one address shared by 3 accounts across 8 transactions", "all 21 transactions on a single day", "one account with 3 or fewer transactions ever", "manual, irregular pacing (about 7.5 minutes between purchases)"], "suggested_action": "review_closer"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.42774	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:04:31.298562+00	0.143891	12688	617	10553	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterat