 separate a shared household or till from a coordinated group, and I cannot see account names, KYC details, who actually owns the two payment methods, or whether the one-day window is real or just the slice of evidence I was given.", "key_signals": ["all 6 accounts used both shared devices and both shared payment methods", "55 transactions compressed into a single day", "very small amounts: 1-50 INR, 1,220 INR total", "gaps of ~5 minutes, consistent with manual use, not a script", "no throwaway accounts - all six have real history"], "suggested_action": "review_closer"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.576906	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:15:00.313723+00	0.11271	12760	2624	31893	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterations": [{"type": "message", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 2624, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4114, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4114}], "input_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available", "output_tokens": 2624, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4114, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": {"web_fetch_requests": 0, "web_search_requests": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 1872}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4114}, "model_usage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 0.111072, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 2, "outputTokens": 2624, "contextWindow": 1000000, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5", "maxOutputTokens": 64000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 8644, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 4114}, "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001": {"costUSD": 0.0016380000000000001, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 1543, "outputTokens": 19, "contextWindow": 200000, "canonicalModel": "claude-haiku-4-5", "maxOutputTokens": 32000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 0}}}
8cd55121-ef45-491e-abf1-4f64741a9caf	65e03a66-6d80-4c3e-99f0-0e3b754cf673	Four accounts made 36 small payments on a single day (28 Aug), all worth 881.85 INR together — amounts from 1 to 50 INR, median about 24 INR. All four used the same two payment instruments, each appearing 17 times, and all four shared one device across 9 transactions, with three of them sharing a second device across 11. That is very heavy sharing: every account touches every instrument. Against that, the behaviour looks manual, not automated — gaps between payments are irregular and average around 4.5 minutes, not machine-fast. The accounts are also established, not throwaways: each has more than three transactions. The money involved is tiny, which cuts both ways: it fits a card-testing probe, but equally fits a family or a small shop's shared tablet and shared cards.	Moderately unsure — the attribute sharing is genuinely unusual and drives almost the whole score, but the tiny amounts, single-day burst, and human-looking timing fit several ordinary explanations just as well.	review_closer	["all 4 accounts used the same two payment instruments, 17 transactions each", "all 4 accounts used one shared device (9 transactions); 3 shared a second (11)", "36 transactions compressed into a single day, 28 Aug 2026", "very small amounts: 1-50 INR, median 24 INR, 881.85 INR total", "timing is irregular and unhurried (median 4.5 min apart) - looks manually driven, not scripted"]	["A household, hostel, or small shop where several people share one or two phones and one or two cards would produce exactly this footprint - and the human-like timing supports that reading.", "Card-testing usually shows failed authorisations and rapid scripted bursts; the evidence given contains no approval/decline outcomes, so this cannot be checked here.", "What would settle it: whether the instruments belong to a cardholder named on any of these accounts, whether the accounts share a registered address or contact details, whether the 28 Aug burst is a one-off or a repeating pattern, and whether these payments went to one merchant or many."]	{"caveats": ["A household, hostel, or small shop where several people share one or two phones and one or two cards would produce exactly this footprint - and the human-like timing supports that reading.", "Card-testing usually shows failed authorisations and rapid scripted bursts; the evidence given contains no approval/decline outcomes, so this cannot be checked here.", "What would settle it: whether the instruments belong to a cardholder named on any of these accounts, whether the accounts share a registered address or contact details, whether the 28 Aug burst is a one-off or a repeating pattern, and whether these payments went to one merchant or many."], "summary": "Four accounts made 36 small payments on a single day (28 Aug), all worth 881.85 INR together — amounts from 1 to 50 INR, median about 24 INR. All four used the same two payment instruments, each appearing 17 times, and all four shared one device across 9 transactions, with three of them sharing a second device across 11. That is very heavy sharing: every account touches every instrument. Against that, the behaviour looks manual, not automated — gaps between payments are irregular and average around 4.5 minutes, not machine-fast. The accounts are also established, not throwaways: each has more than three transactions. The money involved is tiny, which cuts both ways: it fits a card-testing probe, but equally fits a family or a small shop's shared tablet and shared cards.", "confidence": "Moderately unsure — the attribute sharing is genuinely unusual and drives almost the whole score, but the tiny amounts, single-day burst, and human-looking timing fit several ordinary explanations just as well.", "key_signals": ["all 4 accounts used the same two payment instruments, 17 transactions each", "all 4 accounts used one shared device (9 transactions); 3 shared a second (11)", "36 transactions compressed into a single day, 28 Aug 2026", "very small amounts: 1-50 INR, median 24 INR, 881.85 INR total", "timing is irregular and unhurried (median 4.5 min apart) - looks manually driven, not scripted"], "suggested_action": "review_closer"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.574372	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:15:17.134011+00	0.06496	12783	704	12120	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterations": [{"type": "message", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 704, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4137, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4137}], "input_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available", "output_tokens": 704, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4137, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": {"web_fetch_requests": 0, "web_search_requests": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4137}, "model_usage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 0.063302, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 2, "outputTokens": 704, "contextWindow": 1000000, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5", "maxOutputTokens": 64000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 8644, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 4137}, "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001": {"costUSD": 0.001658, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 1558, "outputTokens": 20, "contextWindow": 200000, "canonicalModel": "claude-haiku-4-5", "maxOutputTokens": 32000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 0}}}
